{"session_id":"b49f5c4e-6bf8-4b8d-8ef9-1f7f8fafc561","transcript_path":"/Users/scolladon/.claude/projects/-Users-scolladon-workspace-perso-craft/b49f5c4e-6bf8-4b8d-8ef9-1f7f8fafc561.jsonl","cwd":"/Users/scolladon/workspace/perso/craft","scratchpad_dir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-scolladon-workspace-perso-craft/b49f5c4e-6bf8-4b8d-8ef9-1f7f8fafc561/scratchpad","prompt_id":"833174d3-2f83-41d0-b114-c65eced8f768","permission_mode":"auto","effort":{"level":"high"},"hook_event_name":"Stop","stop_hook_active":false,"last_assistant_message":"PR #71 is merged into main as `e7f39cf`: https://github.com/scolladon/craft/pull/71. Craft now teaches a commit form that bash 3.2 parses, and copilot's guard denies the heredoc commit idiom. That guard denial also closes a way to slip a destructive git command past the guard.\n\n**What bash 3.2 does with the idiom.** I reproduced this with macOS `/bin/bash` 3.2.57; there is no bash 5 on this machine.\n- **Syntax error, no commit:** the body of `git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF' … EOF)\"` holds an odd `'`, `\"` or backtick, or an unbalanced `(`.\n- **Wrong message, exit 0:** an unbalanced `)` ends the message early and runs the rest of the body as shell.\n- **Guard bypass:** `git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'⏎x)\"; git branch -D victim; echo \"⏎EOF⏎)\"` was allowed by copilot's guard, and bash 3.2 then deleted the `victim` branch in a throwaway repo. Plain `git branch -D victim` was denied. zsh treats the whole body as text.\n- **Not tested live:** none of this was run through a real copilot session.\n\n**Decisions:**\n- **Commit form:** you chose to teach it in the core contract. The new `Commit form:` line in `contracts/core.md` says: use `git commit -m \"<message>\"`, one double-quoted `-m` per paragraph. When the message holds `\" $ ` \\ !`, use `git commit -F <file>` instead. Never use the heredoc idiom. This is ADR-605.\n- **Copilot guard:** you chose to deny the idiom. Its special case is deleted, so the guard now refuses it as too complex to verify near git. This is ADR-606 and replaces that part of ADR-595.\n- **Fallback:** `-F <file>` was taken as the design recommended (ADR-607).\n\n**What changed in the review:** one real limit surfaced. On copilot, the `-F` message file can't be written by the file-creation tool outside the working tree. It has to be written by a separate shell command that doesn't mention git. A message that names git and holds `$` or a backtick can't be written that way at all, so it has to be reworded. The copilot README, the gate spec and ADR-607 now say this.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `bash scripts/ci.sh` and the PR's CI were green.\n- Mutation testing on the changed guard code: 13 mutants, 11 killed. The 2 survivors are the ones the code already marked as equivalent before this change.\n- The backlog entry is marked delivered.\n- Metrics and memory are committed to main as `98771b5`. The push used the admin bypass, as expected.\n- The driver log marked this entry timed out after 6 hours, at 2026-10-16 23:14 Paris time. The run still finished; `entry-2.status` says `FINISHED https://github.com/scolladon/craft/pull/71`.\n\n**Next item:** superseding ADR-253 (codex skills loaded by reference, disproven in favour of the symlink route). It is still the top open entry. The prompt is in `.claude/driver-state-bg2/entry-2.next-elected.md`.\n\n**Outside this change:**\n- I added one backlog entry: the copilot \"intended false blocks\" list only mentions `$` with an apostrophe, but the guard denies any `$` or backtick inside quotes.\n- Unproven: the guard allows `=git branch -D victim`. Only zsh would run that as git, and the guard's only user, copilot, runs bash. To prove it: run `zsh -c '=git branch -D victim'` in a throwaway repo.","background_tasks":[],"session_crons":[{"id":"9ed44dc9","schedule":"44 13 * * *","recurring":false,"prompt":"continue craft run bash32-heredoc-commit-idiom (review cycle 2 code awaiting reviewer)"}]}
